            "creator": "jeroenooms",
            "creation_time": "2026-11-15T22:39:50Z",
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "Created attachment 4078\nFix bessel allocation\n\nWith a 4GB address space limit:\n\n  $ ulimit -v 4000000\n  $ Rscript -e 'besselJ(1, 2^64)'\n  Error: cannot allocate vector of size 7.5 Gb\n\ninstead of NaN with a \"too large for bessel_j() algorithm\" warning.\nOn OpenBSD this makes tests/reg-tests-1c.R fail (PR#19175 test),\nbecause OpenBSD refuses the allocation. On Linux it succeeds only\nbecause the memory is never touched.\n\nmath2B() and math3B() in src/main/arithmetic.c size the work array by\nthe largest |nu|, capped at the maximum supported order (1e9 for\nbesselJ/I/K, 1e7 for besselY), i.e. up to 1e9 doubles. But for orders\nabove that maximum, bessel_[jyik]_ex() return NaN without using the\nwork array (also via their nu < 0 branches), so that allocation is\nnever needed.\n\nThe attached patch leaves such orders out of the work-array size.\nResults are unchanged: identical output on Linux for the PR#19175\nexamples and a set of in-range orders, and besselJ(1, 2^64) now runs\nunder the 4GB limit. With the patch, R-devel passes make check-all on\nOpenBSD 8.0.",
